        "id": "the-psychology-of-why-you-stayed",
        "heading": "Understanding Why You Stayed: Releasing the Stigma of Foolishness",
        "content": "\n          <p>To forgive your past self, you must understand the psychological context that governed your decisions at the time:</p>\n          <ul>\n            <li><strong>You Were Operating with Incomplete Life Data:</strong> Higher education is the first time most young adults navigate romantic intimacy, adult boundaries, and manipulative personalities independent of parental supervision. You were an apprentice in human behavior, learning through trial and error.</li>\n            <li><strong>You Fell in Love with Potential, Not Reality:</strong> You stayed because you possessed hope, empathy, and loyalty—noble, beautiful character traits that an unprincipled person exploited. Your mistakes were born of good intentions, not moral stupidity.</li>\n            <li><strong>The Sunk Cost Fallacy:</strong> Having invested months of your university life, shared study hours, and emotional devotion, your brain resisted walking away, desperately hoping that one more conversation would restore the relationship to its original promise.</li>\n          </ul>\n        \n      <p>Recognize that mistakes in university are universal. Millions of graduates look back on their undergraduate romance with a mixture of disbelief and amusement. What feels like an earth-shattering scandal today will become a humorous footnote in your future life story.</p>\n    "
